      <h2>The Good News: Social Security Continues</h2>
      <p>Your Social Security benefits don't stop when you move to Spain. The United States has a Totalization Agreement with Spain, meaning:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>You receive full Social Security benefits abroad indefinitely</li>
        <li>Spain is not a country where benefits are suspended</li>
        <li>You can have payments directed to a Spanish bank account</li>
        <li>There's no age restriction on receiving benefits while abroad</li>
      </ul>
      <p>This is excellent news for American retirees. Many countries restrict payments or require US presence. Spain doesn't.</p>

      <h2>The US-Spain Totalization Agreement Explained</h2>
      <p>This agreement between the US and Spain addresses Social Security taxation and benefits eligibility.</p>

      <h3>What It Does</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Eliminates dual contributions:</strong> You don't pay Social Security tax to both the US and Spain on the same earnings</li>
        <li><strong>Recognizes work credits:</strong> Years worked in Spain count toward US Social Security eligibility (and vice versa)</li>
        <li><strong>Ensures benefit continuity:</strong> You can receive benefits while living in Spain</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Why This Matters</h3>
      <p>If you had some work history in Spain, the agreement allows you to combine US and Spanish work credits toward eligibility. This is rare but beneficial if you qualify. Even if you only worked in the US, the agreement ensures Spain won't tax you twice on Social Security contributions.</p>

      <h2>How to Receive Social Security in Spain</h2>

      <h3>Step 1: Notify Social Security Administration</h3>
      <p>Contact the Social Security Administration before you move (or shortly after):</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Call 1-800-772-1213 (US number, you can call from Spain)</li>
        <li>Visit your local Social Security office before departure</li>
        <li>Complete Form SSA-795 (Statement of Earnings)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Tell them you're moving to Spain and ask about setting up direct deposit.</p>

      <h3>Step 2: Open a Spanish Bank Account</h3>
      <p>You'll need a Spanish bank account to receive direct deposits. See the banking section below.</p>

      <h3>Step 3: Set Up Direct Deposit</h3>
      <p>Provide the Social Security Administration with your Spanish bank details:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>IBAN (International Bank Account Number)</li>
        <li>BIC/SWIFT code</li>
        <li>Spanish address</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Direct deposit takes 2-4 weeks to set up. Your first payment will arrive by wire transfer or direct deposit.</p>

      <h3>Alternative: Wire Transfers</h3>
      <p>If direct deposit isn't working (rare), the Social Security Administration can arrange wire transfers to your Spanish account. Processing time is similar—2-4 weeks.</p>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--yellow">
        <div class="msv__alert-icon">💡</div>
        <div>
          <p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Open your Spanish bank account before you need to set up direct deposit. Banks require NIE and proof of residency, which takes time. Plan ahead.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Social Security Income is Taxable in Spain</h2>
      <p>This is crucial: your Social Security income is fully taxable in Spain. This is different from the US, where some Social Security may be tax-free.</p>

      <h3>How Spain Taxes Social Security</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Ordinary income:</strong> Social Security is taxed as regular income in Spain</li>
        <li><strong>Progressive rates:</strong> 19% to 47% depending on your total income</li>
        <li><strong>Other income matters:</strong> If you have investment income or rental income, it's combined with Social Security to calculate your tax rate</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Example Calculation</h3>
      <p><strong>US retiree receiving $2,000/month Social Security ($24,000/year):</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Spanish marginal tax rate: ~24% on this income level</li>
        <li>Spanish tax owed: ~€5,760/year on Social Security alone</li>
        <li>After tax income: ~€18,240/year (~€1,520/month)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>This is lower than US federal tax would be, but you're paying substantial tax in Spain.</p>

      <h2>The Medicare Problem: It Doesn't Work Abroad</h2>
      <p>This is the #1 surprise for American retirees. Your Medicare coverage stops working when you leave the United States.</p>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--red">
        <div class="msv__alert-icon">⚠️</div>
        <div>
          <p><strong>CRITICAL:</strong> Medicare does NOT cover healthcare outside the US. If you get sick in Spain and rely on Medicare, you'll pay out of pocket. Don't make this mistake. Plan healthcare before you go.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Your Healthcare Options in Spain</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Spanish public healthcare:</strong> Register through your local health centre (free for residents). Quality is excellent</li>
        <li><strong>Private insurance:</strong> €100-250/month depending on age and health</li>
        <li><strong>Combination:</strong> Public healthcare for routine care, private insurance for specialists</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Medicare and Abroad Travel</h3>
      <p>If you plan to visit the US regularly, Medicare still applies during US visits. Your coverage "reactivates" when you're in the US. But while in Spain, it's worthless.</p>

      <h2>When to Claim Social Security: Strategic Timing</h2>
      <p>If you haven't claimed Social Security yet, timing matters. The cost of living in Spain affects your decision.</p>

      <h3>Early Claiming (Age 62)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Reduces benefits by ~30% permanently</li>
        <li>But you start receiving money immediately</li>
        <li>Good if you have limited life expectancy or need income now</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Full Retirement Age (Age 67 for most)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Receive 100% of your benefit</li>
        <li>No reduction, no bonus</li>
        <li>Good if you expect average longevity</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Delayed Claiming (Age 70)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Increases benefits by 24-32% (up to 124% of full amount)</li>
        <li>Requires waiting 5-8 years after full retirement age</li>
        <li>Good if you expect long life expectancy and can live on other income now</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Reporting Requirements</h2>
      <p>You must keep the Social Security Administration informed:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Notify them of any address change (now Spain)</li>
        <li>Report any changes in marital status</li>
        <li>Tell them if you return to the US</li>
        <li>You may need to submit a "statement of residence" occasionally (rare for Spain)</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Spain is a cooperative country with the US, so residence verification is straightforward. You won't face complications.</p>

      <h2>Bank Account Setup</h2>
      <p>To receive Social Security, you need a Spanish bank account. Most banks require:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>NIE (Número de Identidad de Extranjero)</li>
        <li>Passport</li>
        <li>Proof of residency (empadronamiento)</li>
        <li>Proof of income (Social Security statement or pension letter)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Once you have these, opening an account takes 1-2 hours. Cost: free.</p>

