      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="msv__faq">
        <div class="msv__faq-item">
          <div class="msv__faq-q">❓ Can I receive Social Security if I live in Spain?</div>
          <div class="msv__faq-a">Yes. The US-Spain Totalization Agreement allows US citizens to receive Social Security indefinitely while living in Spain. Unlike some countries, Spain is not a country where benefits are suspended. You'll continue receiving full benefits.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__faq-item">
          <div class="msv__faq-q">❓ What is the US-Spain Totalization Agreement?</div>
          <div class="msv__faq-a">This agreement between the US and Spain eliminates dual Social Security taxation and recognizes work credits from both countries toward eligibility. It ensures US workers with some Spanish work credits (and vice versa) can qualify for benefits in their retirement country.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__faq-item">
          <div class="msv__faq-q">❓ How do I receive Social Security payments in Spain?</div>
          <div class="msv__faq-a">You can receive payments via direct deposit to a Spanish bank account, or arrange a wire transfer to your account. Contact the Social Security Administration and provide your Spanish address and Spanish bank details. Processing takes 2-4 weeks.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__faq-item">
          <div class="msv__faq-q">❓ Is Social Security taxable in Spain?</div>
          <div class="msv__faq-a">Yes. Social Security income is fully taxable in Spain as ordinary income. Under the tax treaty, Spain typically gets taxing rights on SS income. The tax rate depends on your other income and ranges from 19% to 47%.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__faq-item">
          <div class="msv__faq-q">❓ Does Medicare work in Spain?</div>
          <div class="msv__faq-a">No. Medicare does not cover healthcare outside the United States. You must obtain Spanish health insurance (public or private) in Spain. Medicare is worthless abroad—don't rely on it.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__faq-item">
          <div class="msv__faq-q">❓ When should I claim Social Security?</div>
          <div class="msv__faq-a">Claiming before your full retirement age (age 67 for most) results in permanently reduced benefits. If you have a long life expectancy, waiting until 70 maximizes lifetime benefits. At 70, benefits are 124% of your full retirement amount. Consult a financial advisor.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
